      <p>Pool excavation is one of the most common jobs BuildHire's machines are booked for across Sydney. Done right, a standard residential pool excavation takes one to two days with the right machine. Done wrong — wrong machine, wrong access plan, wrong soil assessment — it can take twice as long and cost significantly more. This guide is written specifically for pool builders and owner-builders managing their own excavation in Sydney.</p>

      <h2>The Right Machine for Pool Excavation</h2>
      <p>The 5.5T excavator is the standard choice for residential pool excavation in Sydney. A standard 8m × 4m pool at 1,800mm depth requires approximately 57m³ of excavation. The 5.5T's 0.21m³ bucket completes this in roughly 270 passes — achievable in a single day on most sites. The 3,800mm dig depth also provides adequate clearance for deeper pools and allows for the over-excavation needed for formwork and pool shell installation.</p>
      <p>The 3.5T is the alternative for tight access sites — where the gate width is under 1,200mm or the site has limited turning radius. It will take longer (typically 1.5–2 days for a standard pool) but gets the job done where the 5.5T cannot physically fit.</p>

      <h2>Access Requirements: What to Check Before Booking</h2>
      <p>Before confirming your machine booking, check three things:</p>
      <p><strong>Gate width:</strong> The 5.5T requires a minimum gate width of approximately 1,400mm. The 3.5T requires 1,200mm. The 1.7T mini can fit through a 900mm gate. Measure your narrowest access point — not just the gate, but any path, pergola, or overhead obstruction between the street and the pool site.</p>
      <p><strong>Ground conditions:</strong> Soft or wet ground can cause a 5.5T to sink or damage lawns and paving. If the access path is soft, consider laying timber mats or booking a smaller machine. Call BuildHire on 1300 157 882 to discuss — we can advise on ground protection options.</p>
      <p><strong>Overhead clearance:</strong> Check for overhead power lines, pergolas, and eaves along the access path. The 5.5T has a maximum height of approximately 2,700mm in transport position.</p>

      <h2>Sydney Soil Types and Their Impact on Excavation</h2>
      <p>Sydney's geology varies significantly by suburb, and soil type directly affects excavation time and machine selection.</p>
      <p><strong>Western Sydney (Blacktown, Penrith, Campbelltown):</strong> Heavy clay soils that are dense when dry and sticky when wet. Clay is harder to excavate than sandy soil and takes longer to load. A 5.5T is strongly recommended over a 3.5T for clay-heavy sites.</p>
      <p><strong>Northern Beaches and Eastern Suburbs:</strong> Sandstone is common at depth across the Northern Beaches, Manly, and parts of the Eastern Suburbs. Sandstone requires rock-breaking equipment — a hydraulic rock breaker attachment — which BuildHire can discuss on enquiry. Do not assume a standard bucket will handle sandstone.</p>
      <p><strong>Hills District (Castle Hill, Baulkham Hills, Kellyville):</strong> A mix of clay and shale. Shale is softer than sandstone but harder than clay — most 5.5T machines handle it without rock-breaking equipment, but excavation will be slower than in sandy or loam soils.</p>
      <p><strong>Inner West and South Sydney:</strong> Generally sandy loam with some clay — the most straightforward excavation conditions in Sydney. A 5.5T will complete a standard pool in a single day on most Inner West sites.</p>

